Message-ID: <mvWcl3_Cg1Q.UsHTt1Tw_DgJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <mvWcl3_Cg1Q.tMXcjMRKuCgJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <mvWcl3_Cg1Q.tMXcjMRKuCgJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/mvWcl3_Cg1Q/m/UsHTt1Tw_DgJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

It does not make sense to have
      f(id, as.factor(x), model='iid')
because the second argument in f() must be numeric

Also, I think that
       y ~ factor(x)
is more or less the same as
       y ~ f(x, model='iid')
So, by having
       y ~ factor(x) + f(x, model='iid')
we have a redundancy and identification problem as well.

You can see that
   inla(y ~ factor(x))$summary.fix
is similar to
   inla(y ~ f(x, model='iid'))$summary.random$x

